#+startup: noindent

* emacs-elfmt

elfmt formats Emacs Lisp and Lisp data files from the command line.  It uses
Emacs's indentation engine, applies project settings from EditorConfig, and
writes the formatted files in place.  Its intended uses include shell scripts,
formatter runners, and commit hooks.

The implementation lives in ~lisp/elfmt.el~ and requires Emacs 30.1 or later.
EditorConfig support is included in those Emacs versions; Python and an external
EditorConfig command are not required.

** Getting Started

*** Run from Source

From the checkout root:

#+begin_src sh
emacs --quick --script lisp/elfmt.el -- path/to/library.el .dir-locals.el
#+end_src

On systems supporting ~env -S~, the executable source can also be run directly:

#+begin_src sh
./lisp/elfmt.el path/to/library.el
#+end_src

Both forms start a batch Emacs without loading your init file.  Pass one or more
regular files whose Emacs major mode derives from ~emacs-lisp-mode~ or
~lisp-data-mode~.  elfmt does not traverse directories or read standard input.
Except for an optional initial ~--~, arguments are file names.

*** Install with Nix

The flake provides ~packages.x86_64-linux.elfmt~, also exposed as the default
package.  It includes a fixed Emacs executable without graphical support and
installs the ~elfmt~ command.  Build or run it from the checkout:

#+begin_src sh
nix build .#elfmt
./result/bin/elfmt path/to/library.el
nix run .#elfmt -- path/to/library.el
#+end_src

To use it in another flake, add the input:

#+begin_src nix
inputs = {
  elfmt = {
    inputs = {
      nixpkgs = {
        follows = "nixpkgs";
      };
    };

    url = "git+https://github.com/brsvh/emacs-elfmt.git";
  };
};
#+end_src

Select ~inputs.elfmt.packages.${system}.elfmt~ for package lists or formatter
configuration.  The current flake exports ~x86_64-linux~ packages.

** Formatting and Configuration

*** EditorConfig

elfmt resolves ~.editorconfig~ files from each input file's directory and its
parents.  More specific sections and nearer files determine the effective
properties, following EditorConfig's normal rules.  For example:

#+begin_src conf
root = true

[*.el]
charset = utf-8
end_of_line = lf
indent_style = space
indent_size = unset
tab_width = 2
insert_final_newline = true
trim_trailing_whitespace = true
max_line_length = 70
#+end_src

Leaving ~indent_size~ unset preserves Emacs's form-specific indentation.
~indent_style~ controls leading spaces or tabs, and ~tab_width~ controls tab
columns.  EditorConfig applies encoding, line-ending, final-newline, and
trailing-whitespace settings when files are visited and saved.  Whitespace
trimming can also remove trailing spaces inside strings and comments; leave that
property unset when those spaces are significant.

A positive numeric ~max_line_length~ adds a width check after indentation.  Lines
after the first must fit within that many display columns.  The first line is
exempt for file headers or script interpreters.  Omit the property or set it to
~off~ to disable this check.  elfmt does not wrap code, comments, or docstrings to
satisfy the limit, and ~fill-column~ alone does not enable it.

*** Local Indentation Rules

Emacs applies safe file-local and directory-local variables while opening an
input file.  Accepted local indentation variables take precedence over the
corresponding EditorConfig buffer settings.  The explicit leading-whitespace
normalization and width check still use the resolved ~indent_style~ and
~max_line_length~ properties.

elfmt also reads top-level function and macro definitions in the file being
formatted.  It temporarily installs integer and ~defun~ indentation declarations
for that file, then restores the prior symbol properties.  For example:

#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defmacro my-with-resource (resource &rest body)
  "Evaluate BODY while holding RESOURCE."
  (declare (indent 1))
  `(let ((held-resource ,resource))
     ,@body))
#+end_src

This works without loading the input file.  elfmt does not evaluate its forms or
file-local and directory-local ~eval~ settings.  Indentation declarations inside
other forms, function-valued indentation rules, and definitions from other input
files are not imported by this mechanism.

*** Results and Errors

elfmt preserves existing code line breaks and uses Emacs to adjust indentation.
It does not impose a separate line-wrapping or parenthesis-placement style.  It
is a formatter with a width check, not a byte compiler or a complete syntax
validator.  Indentation can depend on the Emacs version; the Nix package pins
that version through its dependency lock.

Progress messages and errors go to standard error.  Exit status is 0 on success
and 1 on a reported formatting error.  Changed formatting by itself is a
successful result.  There is no check-only or standard-output mode.

Files are processed in argument order.  A file is saved after its indentation
and width check succeed.  If that work fails, its on-disk contents are left
unchanged, but files saved earlier in the same invocation remain changed.  elfmt
does not create backup, auto-save, or lock files.

** Other Emacs Lisp Formatters

The following tools offer related workflows.  Their linked documentation
describes their own options and requirements.

- [[https://github.com/emacsmirror/elisp-autofmt][elisp-autofmt]] ::
  Performs broader automatic layout, including line wrapping and parenthesis
  placement.  It offers Emacs commands, formatting on save, and a CLI, using
  Python together with Emacs.  elfmt keeps existing code line breaks and reports
  a configured width violation instead of choosing a new layout.

- [[https://github.com/eschulte/lisp-format][lisp-format]] ::
  Uses Emacs from the command line and follows a clang-format-like interface for
  Lisp languages.  Its ~.lisp-format~ style files contain Emacs Lisp that is
  loaded during formatting.  elfmt focuses on Emacs Lisp and Lisp data, using
  EditorConfig, safe local variables, and source indentation declarations
  without evaluating the input.

- [[https://github.com/tuhdo/semantic-refactor][srefactor-lisp]] ::
  Provides editor commands for formatting a buffer or a definition and
  converting expressions between one-line and multiline forms.  elfmt provides a
  batch, in-place file interface for shell tools.

elfmt's scope is useful when a project wants Emacs indentation and EditorConfig
settings in a CLI workflow while keeping deliberate line breaks.  For automatic
wrapping and expression restructuring, the tools above offer additional
formatting operations.

** Formatter Runners

With ~elfmt~ on ~PATH~, treefmt can pass matching files directly:

#+begin_src toml
[formatter.emacs-lisp]
command = "elfmt"
includes = ["*.el"]
#+end_src

The runner owns file selection and any check-only policy.  For example, ~treefmt
--fail-on-change~ reports files that formatting changed.

